  "course-materials/day1.qmd": {
   "batch": 1,
   "findings": [
    {
     "id": 1,
     "note": null,
     "severity": "blocker",
     "status": "open",
     "text": "'nothing in this file needs cleaning' is false. Daily_AirTemp_Mean_C has zero missing values, but Daily_Precip_Total_mm has 8,002 #N/A entries and other columns are worse. 1c says it correctly: only the column we analyse is clean. The page teaches a wrong lesson about the dataset on the first page a student reads."
    },
    {
     "id": 2,
     "note": null,
     "severity": "major",
     "status": "open",
     "text": "Two counts of the remaining days that cannot both be true: 'the next eight days' on line 18 and 'the next nine days' on line 21."
    },
    {
     "id": 3,
     "note": null,
     "severity": "major",
     "status": "open",
     "text": "The page never mentions f-strings, which 1b calls 'the one thing you should be able to write on your own after today', and which both afternoon sessions then require in boxes headed 'Say it in a sentence (required)'. A student would arrive expecting only to read and copy code."
    },
    {
     "id": 4,
     "note": null,
     "severity": "major",
     "status": "open",
     "text": "'Today we teach one skill' names a different single skill from the one 1b names. Two documents each claim the day's one thing."
    },
    {
     "id": 5,
     "note": null,
     "severity": "major",
     "status": "open",
     "text": "'First we meet Positron' implies a first introduction, but 1a opens 'You have already met Positron in your R course'. Either the page misdescribes the session, or a student who did not take that course gets no warning."
    },
    {
     "id": 6,
     "note": null,
     "severity": "major",
     "status": "open",
     "text": "'By 4:30 you will have imported that file' has no antecedent for 'that file' in its own paragraph. The preceding sentence names weather, not a file."
    },
    {
     "id": 7,
     "note": null,
     "severity": "minor",
     "status": "open",
     "text": "'Today we teach one skill' uses 'we' to mean the instructors, while every earlier 'we' on the page includes the student."
    },
    {
     "id": 8,
     "note": null,
     "severity": "minor",
     "status": "open",
     "text": "'before anybody in the room knows how the individual pieces work' literally includes the instructor."
    },
    {
     "id": 9,
     "note": null,
     "severity": "minor",
     "status": "open",
     "text": "'daily Arctic weather from Toolik Lake in Alaska, running from June 1988 to December 2018' attaches the participle to Alaska."
    },
    {
     "id": 10,
     "note": null,
     "severity": "minor",
     "status": "open",
     "text": "'Playing the whole game means going through the entire thing once' defines the phrase with itself, and 'so that the next eight days have somewhere to attach' leaves both ends of the attachment unnamed."
    },
    {
     "id": 11,
     "note": null,
     "severity": "minor",
     "status": "open",
     "text": "'how to print one' reads first as print(1), which is a plausible first line of Python."
    },
    {
     "id": 12,
     "note": null,
     "severity": "minor",
     "status": "open",
     "text": "'because nothing in this file needs cleaning and Day 4 is the day we spend on cleaning' presents the Day 4 schedule as a reason today's data is clean."
    }
   ],
